(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_chemistry_somebasicconceptsofchemistry=[{id:"neet-2026-q049",pn:49,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry",st:"Gas volume stoichiometry",q:"When 1 dm\xB3 of CO\u2082 gas is passed over hot coke the volume of gaseous mixture after complete reaction at STP becomes 1.4 dm\xB3. The composition of the gaseous mixture at STP is:",o:["0.6 dm\xB3 of CO, 0.8 dm\xB3 of CO\u2082","0.8 dm\xB3 of CO, 0.8 dm\xB3 of CO\u2082","0.8 dm\xB3 of CO, 0.6 dm\xB3 of CO\u2082","0.6 dm\xB3 of CO, 0.4 dm\xB3 of CO\u2082"],c:2,s:`The reaction is CO\u2082 + C(s) \u2192 2CO.
Let x dm\xB3 of CO\u2082 react. Then CO\u2082 left = 1 \u2013 x and CO formed = 2x.
Total volume = (1 \u2013 x) + 2x = 1 + x = 1.4, so x = 0.4 dm\xB3.
Volume of CO\u2082 = 1 \u2013 0.4 = 0.6 dm\xB3.
Volume of CO = 2 \xD7 0.4 = 0.8 dm\xB3.`},{id:"neet-2026-q065",pn:65,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry",st:"Mole concept",q:`The number of hydrogen atoms present in 5.4 g of urea is:
(Given: Molar mass of urea : 60 g mol\u207B\xB9, N_A : 6.022 \xD7 10\xB2\xB3 particles mol\u207B\xB9)`,o:["2.168 \xD7 10\xB2\xB3","2.168 \xD7 10\xB2\xB2","1.084 \xD7 10\xB2\xB2","1.084 \xD7 10\xB2\xB3"],c:0,s:`Urea is H\u2082N\u2013CO\u2013NH\u2082, so one molecule has 4 H atoms.
Moles of urea = 5.4/60 = 0.09.
H atoms = 0.09 \xD7 4 \xD7 6.022 \xD7 10\xB2\xB3 = 2.1679 \xD7 10\xB2\xB3.
This is about 2.168 \xD7 10\xB2\xB3.`,sfig:{w:170,h:58,alt:"Urea: H\u2082N\u2013C\u2013NH\u2082 with O double-bonded above the central C.",d:[["t",90,22,"O","m"],["l",87.5,26,87.5,38],["l",92.5,26,92.5,38],["t",90,52,"C","m"],["t",82,52,"H\u2082N \u2013","e"],["t",98,52,"\u2013 NH\u2082",""]]}},{id:"neet-2026r-q075",pn:75,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry",st:"Redox titration normality",q:"In an acidic medium, 10 mL of 0.25 M oxalic acid is titrated with KMnO\u2084 solution. If the volume of KMnO\u2084 solution required to reach end point is 10 mL, the strength of the KMnO\u2084 solution is",o:["0.20 M","0.25 M","0.15 M","0.10 M"],c:3,s:`Reaction: KMnO\u2084 + H\u2082C\u2082O\u2084 \u2192 Mn\xB2\u207A + 2CO\u2082 + H\u2082O (acidic medium).
At the end point, equivalents of KMnO\u2084 = equivalents of H\u2082C\u2082O\u2084.
Mn\u2077\u207A \u2192 Mn\xB2\u207A gains 5 electrons, oxalic acid loses 2 electrons.
10 \xD7 5 \xD7 M = 10 \xD7 0.25 \xD7 2
M = 5/50 = 1/10 = 0.10 M`},{id:"neet-2026r-q088",pn:88,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry",st:"Stoichiometry, combustion",q:`The amount of carbon dioxide evolved upon complete combustion of 116 g of n-butane is
(Given: atomic mass in amu H = 1, C = 12 and O = 16)`,o:["322 g","176 g","362 g","352 g"],c:3,s:`C\u2084H\u2081\u2080 + 13/2 O\u2082 \u2192 4CO\u2082 + 5H\u2082O.
1 mole of C\u2084H\u2081\u2080 produces 4 mole of CO\u2082.
58 g C\u2084H\u2081\u2080 gives 4 \xD7 44 g CO\u2082.
So 116 g C\u2084H\u2081\u2080 gives (4 \xD7 44 / 58) \xD7 116 = 352 g CO\u2082.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q089",pn:89,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry",st:"Rounding off and significant figures",q:"The numbers 17.0145 and 21.0235 were rounded to three figures after the decimal point. The resulting numbers, respectively, are",o:["17.015 and 21.023","17.014 and 21.024","17.015 and 21.024","17.014 and 21.023"],c:1,s:`Rule: if the digit to be removed is 5, the preceding digit stays the same if it is even.
If the preceding digit is odd, it is increased by one.
In 17.0145 the preceding digit is 4 (even), so it becomes 17.014.
In 21.0235 the preceding digit is 3 (odd), so it becomes 21.024.`},{id:"neet-2025-q054",pn:54,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry",st:"Dalton's atomic theory",q:"Dalton's Atomic theory could not explain which of the following?",o:["Law of conservation of mass","Law of constant proportion","Law of multiple proportion","Law of gaseous volume"],c:3,s:`Dalton's theory could explain the laws of chemical combination.
However, it could not explain the law of gaseous volumes.`},{id:"neet-2025-q068",pn:68,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry",st:"Mole concept, number of atoms",q:`Among the following, choose the ones with equal number of atoms.
A. 212 g of Na\u2082CO\u2083(s) [molar mass = 106 g]
B. 248 g of Na\u2082O(s) [molar mass = 62 g]
C. 240 g of NaOH(s) [molar mass = 40 g]
D. 12 g of H\u2082(g) [molar mass = 2 g]
E. 220 g of CO\u2082(g) [molar mass = 44 g]
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A, B, and C only","A, B, and D only","B, C, and D only","B, D, and E only"],c:1,s:`Number of atoms = (given mass / molar mass) \xD7 atomicity \xD7 N_A
A. (212/106) \xD7 6 \xD7 N_A = 12 N_A
B. (248/62) \xD7 3 \xD7 N_A = 12 N_A
C. (240/40) \xD7 3 \xD7 N_A = 18 N_A
D. (12/2) \xD7 N_A \xD7 2 = 12 N_A
E. (220/44) \xD7 N_A \xD7 3 = 15 N_A
A, B and D have the same number of atoms.`},{id:"neet-2024-q055",pn:55,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry",st:"Limiting reagent and molarity",q:"1 gram of sodium hydroxide was treated with 25 mL of 0.75 M HCl solution, the mass of sodium hydroxide left unreacted is equal to",o:["Zero mg","200 mg","750 mg","250 mg"],c:3,s:`Molarity M = (W \xD7 1000)/(M\u2082 \xD7 V in mL), so W = (M \xD7 M\u2082 \xD7 V)/1000.
Mass of HCl = (0.75 \xD7 36.5 \xD7 25)/1000 = 0.684 g.
HCl + NaOH \u2192 NaCl + H\u2082O; 36.5 g HCl reacts with 40 g NaOH.
0.684 g HCl reacts with (40/36.5) \xD7 0.684 \u2248 0.750 g NaOH.
NaOH left = 1 g \u2212 0.750 g = 0.250 g = 250 mg.`},{id:"neet-2024-q083",pn:83,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry",st:"Mole concept, number of atoms",q:"The highest number of helium atoms is in",o:["4 g of helium","2.271098 L of helium at STP","4 mol of helium","4 u of helium"],c:2,s:`(1) 4 g of He = 4/4 = 1 mol = N_A atoms.
(2) 2.2710982 L at STP = 2.271/22.710982 = 0.1 mol = 0.1 N_A atoms.
(3) 4 mol of He = 4 N_A atoms.
(4) 4 u of He = 4/4 = 1 atom.
The largest number of atoms is in 4 mol.`},{id:"neet-2024-q086",pn:86,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry",st:"Empirical formula",q:`A compound X contains 32% of A, 20% of B and remaining percentage of C. Then, the empirical formula of X is :
(Given atomic masses of A = 64; B = 40; C = 32 u)`,o:["AB\u2082C\u2082","ABC\u2084","A\u2082BC\u2082","ABC\u2083"],c:3,s:`Percentage of C = 100 \u2013 32 \u2013 20 = 48%.
Moles of A = 32/64 = 1/2.
Moles of B = 20/40 = 1/2.
Moles of C = 48/32 = 3/2.
Divide by the smallest (1/2): A : B : C = 1 : 1 : 3.
Empirical formula = ABC\u2083.`,sfig:{w:405,h:130,alt:"Table: A 32 percent, 32/64 = 1/2, (1/2)\xD72, 1; B 20 percent, 20/40 = 1/2, (1/2)\xD72, 1; C 48 percent, 48/32 = 3/2, (3/2)\xD72, 3.",d:[["r",10,10,386,118],["l",58,10,58,128],["l",144,10,144,128],["l",220,10,220,128],["l",320,10,320,128],["l",10,44,396,44],["l",10,72,396,72],["l",10,100,396,100],["t",34,31,"Element","m"],["t",101,25,"Mass","m"],["t",101,38,"percentage %","m"],["t",182,25,"No. of","m"],["t",182,38,"moles","m"],["t",270,25,"No. of moles/","m"],["t",270,38,"Smallest number","m"],["t",358,25,"Simple whole","m"],["t",358,38,"number","m"],["t",34,62,"A","m"],["t",101,62,"32%","m"],["t",182,62,"32/64 = 1/2","m"],["t",270,62,"(1/2) \xD7 2","m"],["t",358,62,"= 1","m"],["t",34,90,"B","m"],["t",101,90,"20%","m"],["t",182,90,"20/40 = 1/2","m"],["t",270,90,"(1/2) \xD7 2","m"],["t",358,90,"= 1","m"],["t",34,118,"C","m"],["t",101,118,"48%","m"],["t",182,118,"48/32 = 3/2","m"],["t",270,118,"(3/2) \xD7 2","m"],["t",358,118,"= 3","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2023-q057",pn:57,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry",st:"Stoichiometry (mass-mass)",q:`The right option for the mass of CO\u2082 produced by heating 20 g of 20 % pure limestone is (Atomic mass of Ca = 40)
[CaCO\u2083 --1200 K--> CaO + CO\u2082]`,o:["1.32 g","1.12 g","1.76 g","2.64 g"],c:2,s:`CaCO\u2083 \u2192 CaO + CO\u2082.
100 g CaCO\u2083 gives 44 g CO\u2082.
Pure CaCO\u2083 = 20 \xD7 20/100 = 4 g.
CO\u2082 from 4 g = (44/100) \xD7 4 = 1.76 g.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q052",pn:52,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry",st:"Significant figures",q:"The density of the solution is 2.15 g mL\u207B\xB9, then mass of 2.5 mL solution in correct significant figures is :",o:["53.75 g","5375 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 g","5.4 g","5.38 g"],c:2,s:`In multiplication or division, the answer keeps as many significant figures as the number that has the fewest.
Mass = density \xD7 volume = 2.15 \xD7 2.5 = 5.375.
2.5 has only 2 significant figures, so round to 5.4 g.`},{id:"neet-2022-q068",pn:68,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry",st:"Stoichiometry with purity",q:`What mass of 95% pure CaCO\u2083 will be required to neutralise 50 mL of 0.5 M HCl solution according to the following reaction?
CaCO\u2083(s) + 2HCl(aq) \u2192 CaCl\u2082(aq) + CO\u2082(g) + 2H\u2082O(l)
[Calculate upto second place of decimal point]`,o:["1.32 g","3.65 g","9.50 g","1.25 g"],c:0,s:`Let m g of the impure sample be taken.
Pure CaCO\u2083 = (95/100) \xD7 m g.
Moles of CaCO\u2083 = (95/100) \xD7 (m/100).
Moles of HCl needed = 2 \xD7 moles of CaCO\u2083.
Moles of HCl present = (50/1000) \xD7 0.5.
So 2 \xD7 (95/100) \xD7 (m/100) = (50/1000) \xD7 0.5.
m = 1.315 g \u2248 1.32 g.`},{id:"neet-2021-q082",pn:82,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry",st:"Empirical formula",q:"An organic compound contains 78% (by wt.) carbon and remaining percentage of hydrogen. The right option for the empirical formula of this compound is : [Atomic wt. of C is 12, H is 1]",o:["CH\u2084","CH","CH\u2082","CH\u2083"],c:3,s:`Take 100 g: C = 78 g, H = 22 g.
Moles C = 78/12 = 6.5. Moles H = 22/1 = 22.
Divide by 6.5: C = 1, H = 22/6.5 = 3.38.
The nearest simple whole number ratio is 1 : 3, so the empirical formula is CH\u2083.`}]})();
